digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Collective cell migration is a hallmark of morphogenesis, tissue homeostasis, and wound healing."];
  "N10" [label="(8,1,0) Changes in membrane properties significantly influence leading edge dynamics in single cells."];
  "N17" [label="(11,1,0) Protein-independent signaling cascades originating from within the plasma membrane are suitable for initiating sustained changes in cell dynamics."];
  "N18" [label="(12,1,0) It is evident that membrane mechanics regulate the dynamics of individual cells."];
  "N19" [label="(12,2,0) The contribution of plasma membrane-derived signals to collective cell migration remains unclear when independent of the direct link between adhesions and cytoskeleton."];
  "N20" [label="(13,1,0) There is a lack of tools capable of dissecting the mechanical cues at the plasma membrane from the mechano-transduction through cell-cell adhesion molecules."];
  "N23" [label="(16,3,0) Artificial photoswitchable cell-cell adhesions based on Cph1 do not directly link to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "N30" [label="(21,1,0) Mechanical coupling of cells at their membranes via artificial adhesion proteins that do not link directly to the actin cytoskeleton induces increased membrane tension."];
  "N31" [label="(21,2,0) Increased membrane tension is sufficient to achieve collective cell migration."];
  "N4" [label="(3,2,0) α-catenin and β-catenin recruit to the cytoplasmic tail, forming a mechanosensitive link to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to investigate whether selectively increasing cell-cell membrane coupling with photoswitchable, cytoskeleton-independent adhesion proteins can raise membrane tension and drive collective cell migration through a membrane-originated signaling pathway, including testing the involvement of PLD2-PA-mTOR signaling.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A clean paper-level synthesis should connect the motivation for isolating plasma-membrane cues, the use of photoswitchable cytoskeleton-independent adhesion proteins, and the proposed membrane-tension route to collective migration while treating downstream PLD2-PA-mTOR signaling as a pathway to be tested rather than as established by the repaired premises."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Plasma-membrane property changes influence leading-edge and migration behavior in single cells through multiple mechanisms and can initiate sustained changes in cell dynamics."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Because collective cell migration is important in morphogenesis, tissue homeostasis, and wound healing, it is meaningful to investigate factors that control collective versus individual migration behavior."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Cell-cell adhesion molecules participate in mechanotransduction through cytoplasmic components that form mechanosensitive links to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because membrane mechanics regulate individual cell dynamics and plasma-membrane changes can alter migration behavior, it is motivated to investigate whether membrane-based signaling also contributes to collective cell dynamics."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) The unclear contribution of plasma-membrane-derived signals to collective migration, together with the lack of tools separating membrane cues from adhesion-linked mechanotransduction, motivates developing or using tools that isolate membrane-based cues from cytoskeletal coupling."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The study aims to test the relevance of membrane-based signaling for collective cell dynamics using approaches that separate plasma-membrane cues from direct cytoskeletal mechanotransduction."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Artificial photoswitchable Cph1-based cell-cell adhesions that do not directly link to actin are suitable candidate tools for probing membrane-based signaling without direct cytoskeletal coupling."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Artificial membrane coupling through adhesion proteins that lack direct actin linkage provides a plausible route to increased membrane tension and potentially collective migration."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Photoswitchable Cph1-based adhesions lacking direct actin linkage can be used to test whether cytoskeleton-independent cell-cell membrane coupling increases membrane tension."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Membrane tension is a plausible membrane-originated mediator linking cytoskeleton-independent artificial cell-cell coupling to collective migration, but the premises do not establish that membrane tension alone is the exclusive cause."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The role of plasma-membrane-derived cues in collective cell migration remains unresolved because their contribution independent of direct adhesion-cytoskeleton linkage is unclear and tools to separate these cues are lacking."];
  "N10" -> "R1" [label="induction-case"];
  "N17" -> "R1" [label="induction-common"];
